# Fret - electric guitars and basses.
#
# A string plucked by a pick, a finger or a slapping thumb, heard through
# its pickups: a magnet under the string at the neck, at the bridge or both,
# a single coil or a humbucker, and the tone knob. A pickup hears the string
# where it sits, so the neck is round and the bridge thin and bright; a
# quarter of the way along, the 4th harmonic is gone.
#
# The hands: `mute` rests the palm by the bridge (the mod wheel does too),
# `harmonic` touches the 12th, 7th or 5th fret, `buzz` lets the frets slap,
# `strum` spreads a chord across the strings, and with one voice `slide`
# glides from note to note. `drive` is the amp, with a speaker as it comes
# in, and `feedback` lets it shake the held strings until a note sustains.
#
# The string's partials sit within a few cents of the harmonic series: the
# loop's DC blocker sits at a hundredth of the note rather than a tenth, so
# its phase lead doesn't pull every partial flat.
#
# Seventy-four patches in eight families, plainest first:
#
#   clean    Init, the pickups, chorus, twelve strings, baritone, harmonics (12)
#   crunch   an amp breaking up, power chords, tremolo, fuzz, funk (9)
#   lead     driven leads, feedback, echo, fuzz, the whammy bar (10)
#   strum    strummed chords, slow ripples, twelve strings (8)
#   jazz     archtops: comping, chord melody, octaves, a picked line (7)
#   steel    lap and pedal steel, swelled chords, a crying lead (7)
#   bass     finger, pick, slap, five strings, fretless, dub (14)
#   odd      drones, chimes, buzzing frets, a broken radio, slow swells (7)
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are numbered
# as in Fret.h's MacroDest: #1 tone, #2 hardness, #3 position, #4 mute,
# #5 sustain, #6 bright, #7 buzz, #8 strum, #9 slide, #10 vibrato, #11 drive,
# #12 feedback, #14 volume, #15 ring, #16 swell, #17 pitch (one semitone is
# 0.0417). Where a macro at full moves the level by more than 1.5 dB, a
# volume route takes it back out, as far as the volume knob goes. Palm drops
# up to 3 dB where the knob runs out or a compressor sits after it, so Funk's
# Palm and Rattle also soften the pick. Through Chug's amp a volume route does
# nothing, so Tight and Open trade mute against pick hardness instead.
# Swell is left quiet on purpose: the note arrives later. Howl only goes on patches driven past 0.5, since feedback
# without drive just gets louder.
#
# `swell` fades each note in from silence over that many seconds, as a
# volume knob rolled up after the pick. A note shorter than its swell never
# reaches its level, so the swelled patches are slow ones.

machine Fret
role tune

patch Init family=clean note=52 range=40..88

patch "Clean Neck" family=clean note=52 range=40..88
  # The neck pickup alone: round and warm.
  set macro1 "Dark"
  set macro2 "Bright"
  set macro3 "Dig In"
  set macro4 "Palm"
  set macro5 "Ring Out"
  set macro6 "Vibrato"
  set macro7 "Grit"
  set macro8 "Swell"
